		heading: "Where the verse sits",
		bodyHtml:
			"<p>Haggai speaks to people who had come back from exile and then stalled. The word of our Father reaches them with a complaint about their priorities: &ldquo;These people say it isn&rsquo;t yet time to rebuild the house of our Father.&rdquo; (Haggai 1:2)</p><p>Verse 8 is the answer to that excuse. It is a command that arrives after a question and before an explanation.</p>",
	},
	{
		id: "paneled",
		heading: "Paneled houses and a house in ruins",
		bodyHtml:
			"<p>The question comes first: &ldquo;Should you be living in your paneled houses while this house lies in ruins?&rdquo; (Haggai 1:4) The people had found time and money for their own comfortable homes while the house of our Father stayed unbuilt. The charge is not that they had houses. It is that they had put everything else first and called it &ldquo;not yet time.&rdquo;</p><p>Our Father then asks them to look at their own results: &ldquo;Consider carefully how you have lived.&rdquo; (Haggai 1:5) The next verse describes the pattern: &ldquo;You have sown much, but harvested little.&rdquo; (Haggai 1:6) Effort without enough to show for it is the evidence he points to.</p>",
	},
	{
		id: "verse8",
		heading: "What Haggai 1:8 actually says",
		bodyHtml:
			"<p>Then comes the command: &ldquo;Go up into the hills, bring down timber, and rebuild the house, so that I may take pleasure in it and be honored,&rdquo; says our Father. (Haggai 1:8)</p><p>Three parts of it are worth slowing down for.</p><ul><li><strong>Go up into the hills, bring down timber.</strong> The task is practical and physical. Our Father does not ask for a feeling or a vow; he asks for lumber to be cut and carried.</li><li><strong>Rebuild the house.</strong> In the context of the chapter, &ldquo;the house&rdquo; is the house of our Father that still lay in ruins (Haggai 1:2, Haggai 1:9).</li><li><strong>So that I may take pleasure in it and be honored.</strong> The reason is not that he lacks a place to be. The reason is relationship: he takes pleasure in what his people build for him, and he is honored by it.</li></ul>",
	},
	{
		id: "why",
		heading: "Why the harvests failed",
		bodyHtml:
			"<p>Verse 9 gives the diagnosis in our Father&rsquo;s own words: &ldquo;Because of my house, which still lies in ruins, while each of you is busy with his own house.&rdquo; (Haggai 1:9) Verse 10 adds that &ldquo;the heavens have withheld their dew, and the earth has withheld its produce.&rdquo; (Haggai 1:10)</p><p>It is a specific word to a specific community, not a promise that building anything religious guarantees a good year.</p>",
	},
	{
		id: "house",
		heading: "How the New Testament reads &ldquo;the house&rdquo;",
		bodyHtml:
			"<p>Many readers search &ldquo;Haggai 1:8 meaning&rdquo; hoping for a personal application, and the New Testament does give one, though it is careful about where it comes from. It speaks of our Father&rsquo;s house as people. Paul writes: &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t you know you are our Father&rsquo;s temple, and our Father&rsquo;s Spirit lives in you?&rdquo; (1 Corinthians 3:16)</p><p>Peter uses the same building language: &ldquo;You too are being built, like living stones, into a spiritual house and a holy priesthood&rdquo; (1 Peter 2:5) And Paul says of the whole family: &ldquo;in him, you also are being built together into a home for our Father in the Holy Spirit.&rdquo; (Ephesians 2:22)</p><p>So the line from Haggai to today is this: our Father still wants a house in which he is honored, and in Christ that house is a people being built together. The honest limit is that Haggai 1:8 itself commands the rebuilding of a physical house. The New Testament passages explain what the house became; they do not erase what Haggai first said.</p>",
	},
	{
		id: "today",
		heading: "What it asks of us now",
		bodyHtml:
			"<p>Read in that light, the verse asks a question rather than demanding a program: what have we been calling &ldquo;not yet time&rdquo;? The people in Haggai were busy, not rebellious. Our Father&rsquo;s answer was to name the real cost of putting his house last and to invite them back to work he would take pleasure in.</p><p>If you want the longer story of stones, sons and the house our Father is building, read <a href=\"/blog/from-stones-to-sons/\" style=\"color: var(--color-brand);\" class=\"hover:underline\">From Stones to Sons</a>.</p>",
	},
];

